Developers like that because it helps them catch mismatches in their object prototypes. It's sort of like object scripting on steroids, except it actually helps you find errors before your code is parsed by the JavaScript engine and fails at runtime.

If you take *any* JavaScript class, you run the risk of someone violating duck typing: [https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/typeof](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/typeof). Duck typing is when you expect an object to have a certain attribute or property, but you can't tell with the `typeof` operator. It can lead to errors that are very bad for user experience.

For example:
```typescript
class Pet {}
interface Animal {
    breed: string,
    name: string,
}
class Cat extends Pet implements Animal {}
class Dog extends Pet implements Animal {}
let pets: Pet[] = [];
pets.push(pets[0]); // Violates *duck scripting* because `pets[0]` is `undefined`.
class Fish extends Pet {} // This also violates duck typing.
```
