In TypeScript, the `as` operator can be used to assert a type, which tells the compiler "confidently believe that the type variable is the specified type, even though the compiler might not be able to prove that it's the specified type without extra information.". You can use the `as typeof` notation to disambiguate the assertion from other uses of the `as` operator, such as casting a value to a new value. For example, if you have a variable `a` with type `number | undefined`, you can assert its type to `never` if you know it will be `undefined`.

```typescript
let a: number | undefined;

if (a === undefined) {
  a = a as typeof a & { never: never };
}

let b: number | string | boolean = true;
b = b as typeof b & number;
```

By using the `as typeof` notation, you're telling the compiler that the variable `a` has no possible values and is always `undefined`. This is called an "exhaustive assertion". Similarly, for the variable `b`, you're asserting that it can only hold `true` or `0`, not `false` or `'string'`.

Keep in mind that using the `as typeof` notation is more of a "developer assertion" than a "runtime type guard", meaning that the TypeScript compiler will not check whether the value actually matches the assertion at runtime. It's there to make the code easier to understand and maintain without sacrificing performance.

