Here's an example of how a Table of Contents (ToC) can be generated automatically in a LaTeX document using the `\tableofcontents` command:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{lipsum} % For dummy text

\begin{document}

\title{My First LaTeX Document}
\author{Your Name Here}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\lipsum[1]

\section{First Section}
\lipsum[2-3]

\section{Second Section}
\lipsum[4]

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `\tableofcontents` command is placed after the `\maketitle` command. When you compile this document, LaTeX will generate the ToC with the calculated section levels, but no page numbers. You can modify the `\tableofcontents` command to include page numbers using the `\addtotoc` command.

The `\addtotoc` command can be used to customize the titles and headers of the ToC:

```latex
\tableofcontents\addtotoc{Introduction}
\tableofcontents\addtotoc[{First Section}]{First Section}
\tableofcontents\addtotoc[0]{Second Section}
```

In this example, `\addtotoc{Introduction}` will add "Introduction" to the ToC, `\addtotoc[{First Section}]{First Section}` will add "First Section" to the ToC with the title "First Section" in curly braces, `\addtotoc[0]{Second Section}` will add "Second Section" to the ToC with no header.

If you want to add numbers to the sections, you can use the `\setcounter{secnumdepth}{<value>}` command before your `\tableofcontents` command, where `<value>` is the number of subsection levels that you want to be numbered. For example, setting it to -1 will number the sections but not the subsections.

