The `==` operator can sometimes cause unexpected behavior when comparing strings. For example, consider the following code:

```
String str1 = "Ajay";
String str2 = new String("Ajay");

if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("Both strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Both strings are not equal");
}
```

In this case, `str1` and `str2` appear to have the same value, but `str1 == str2` returns `false` because `==` compares the memory addresses of the two objects, which are different even though the objects have the same value.

In summary, you should use the `==` operator when comparing primitive data types like `int`, `double`, `boolean`, etc., and the `equals()` method when comparing objects like `String`.

